# Line endings.
#
# Windows batch files MUST be CRLF. cmd.exe seeks by byte offset between
# commands rather than reading line by line, so an LF-only .bat makes it land
# mid-line and execute the tail of one as a command. mutate.bat failed on its
# own documented example that way, with an error naming neither the script nor
# the line endings, and verify.bat next to it ran fine - so the one comparison
# that looked decisive pointed away from the cause.
#
# Everything else is normalised to LF in the repository, which is what the
# tree already holds.

* text=auto

*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

*.sh  text eol=lf
